# SDOC Slide Layouts @layouts-example
{
    @meta
    {
        type: slides

        title: SDOC Slide Layouts

        company: SDOC
    }

    @about
    {
        Every slide layout, every variant and every slide property, with
        neutral content — a theme can be checked against the whole set at
        once. Build it:

        `node tools/build-slides.js examples/layouts-example.sdoc`

        Add `--theme path/to/theme` to check a theme of your own against it,
        and `--check` to confirm nothing has overflowed its slide.

        Three slides carry `:detail` children, so Down and Up exercise the
        vertical navigation; one of those details is marked `optional: true`,
        so `--pdf`, `--pptx` and `--no-optional` all drop it while the plain
        HTML build keeps it.
    }

    # SDOC Slides @cover
    {
        config: title

        kicker: Layout reference

        status: Build it, then build it again with your own theme

        Structured layouts for decks that carry an argument.
    }

    # One statement, nothing else @divider
    {
        config: section

        kicker: Section

        A section divider states the turn the argument is about to take.
    }

    # Three figures, three captions @stats-slide
    {
        config: stats

        kicker: Stats

        lede: The stats layout sets a figure against a small caption.

        # 3.2 s @stat-a
        {
            Median cold start, before
        }

        # 180 ms @stat-b
        {
            Median cold start, after
        }

        # 18× @stat-c
        {
            The ratio the slide is about
        }

        # Where the time went @stat-detail :detail
        {
            config: rows

            kicker: Detail

            lede: A detail slide is a slide. It reads its own `config:` line, so a drilldown can carry any layout.

            # Cold module graph @sd-1
            {
                value: 2.4 s

                Resolved on every boot, then cached.
            }

            # Config parsing @sd-2
            {
                value: 0.6 s

                Replaced with a precomputed manifest.
            }

            # Everything else @sd-3
            {
                value: 0.2 s

                Unchanged.
            }
        }
    }

    # Two paths, one of them shorter @pipeline-slide
    {
        config: pipeline

        kicker: Pipeline

        lede: A bold step is marked and takes the row's accent. Marking is semantic: it says this step is required, and the theme decides what that looks like.

        # The long way @path-a
        {
            accent: primary

            {[.]
                - Request
                - **Encode**
                - Queue
                - **Decode**
                - Handler
            }
        }

        # The short way @path-b
        {
            accent: secondary

            {[.]
                - Request
                - **Dispatch**
                - Handler
            }
        }
    }

    # Numbered columns @columns-slide
    {
        config: columns

        numbered: true

        kicker: Columns

        # First @col-1
        {
            Columns take one child scope each. Adding `numbered: true` prefixes
            them 01, 02, 03.
        }

        # Second @col-2
        {
            A column holds anything a slide holds: prose, a list, a table, an
            image, a nested block.
        }

        # Third @col-3
        {
            Four or more columns tighten the gutter automatically.
        }
    }

    # Cards @card-slide
    {
        config: columns

        variant: card

        kicker: Columns, card variant

        lede: A card rules its heading in the slide's accent. `caption:` sets a line under the column.

        # Ingest @card-1
        {
            caption: Step one

            Cards suit a figure row: a heading, a line of body, a caption
            underneath.
        }

        # Index @card-2
        {
            caption: Step two

            An image at the top of each column makes the standard figure row.
        }

        # Serve @card-3
        {
            caption: Step three

            The caption sets smaller and greyer than the body.
        }
    }

    # People @people-slide
    {
        config: columns

        variant: people

        kicker: Columns, people variant

        lede: The people variant wraps onto a second row and sets the caption as an accented role.

        # Avery Lindqvist @person-1
        {
            caption: Engineering

            Wrote the parser and still maintains it.
        }

        # Rowan Okafor @person-2
        {
            caption: Design

            Owns the theme system.
        }

        # Sasha Beaumont @person-3
        {
            caption: Research

            Measures what the format costs to read.
        }

        # Kit Nakamura @person-4
        {
            caption: Operations

            Keeps the release pipeline honest.
        }
    }

    # Panels @panel-slide
    {
        config: columns

        variant: panel

        kicker: Columns, panel variant

        # Patent @panel-1
        {
            kicker: Patent

            Hard-edged panels divided by a single-pixel seam. Accents alternate
            unless a column sets its own.
        }

        # Software @panel-2
        {
            kicker: Software

            Dense copy can set in a second, narrower face.
        }

        # Data @panel-3
        {
            kicker: Data

            Five panels is the practical limit at this measure.
        }

        # Standard @panel-4
        {
            kicker: Standard

            A column can set `accent:` to override the alternation.
        }

        # Pathway @panel-5
        {
            kicker: Pathway

            The panel variant suits a set of parallel assets.
        }
    }

    # A comparison matrix @matrix-slide
    {
        config: matrix

        kicker: Matrix

        highlight: last

        footnote: A footnote sets small and grey under the body, for provenance and caveats.

        {[table]
            Who | Regime | Conversion | What they build | How we differ
            **Alpha Systems** *Series C* | Watts+ | Yes | A managed service with a proprietary runtime | More capital, a longer runway
            **Beta Compute** *shipping* | Watts | Yes | An appliance with an on-device runtime | The strongest comparable in the set
            **Gamma Micro** *public* | Milliwatts | n/a | A general-purpose part, no specialisation | The closest public comparator
            **This company** *raising* | Milliwatts and below | No | One path, no conversion step in it | The only row with no in the conversion column
        }
    }

    # Numbered rows @rows-slide
    {
        config: rows

        numbered: true

        kicker: Rows

        # Tools and simulator @row-1
        {
            value: Layer 1

            A row carries an index, a label, a body and a right-hand value. Any
            of the four may be omitted.
        }

        # Application engineering @row-2
        {
            value: Layer 2

            Rows separate with hairlines rather than boxes, which keeps a dense
            list legible at projection distance.
        }

        # Licensed IP @row-3
        {
            value: Layer 3

            The `variant: mono` form turns the label into a monospace accent —
            a date, a process node, a stage — which reads as a timeline.
        }
    }

    # A ledger @ledger-slide
    {
        config: rows

        variant: ledger

        kicker: Rows, ledger variant

        lede: The ledger variant drops the body and leaves a label against a figure.

        # Storage @ledger-1
        {
            value: 12.4 GB
        }

        # Index @ledger-2
        {
            value: 3.1 GB
        }

        # Cache @ledger-3
        {
            value: 0.9 GB
        }

        # How the figures are counted @ledger-detail :detail
        {
            optional: true

            kicker: Detail, optional

            Marked `optional: true`, so it is here while you present and absent
            from the PDF and the PPTX. Pass `--with-optional` to keep it, or
            `--no-optional` to drop the optional slides from the HTML too.

            Measured after compaction, excluding replicas.
        }
    }

    # Split, with a timeline @split-slide
    {
        config: split

        kicker: Split

        weights: 48 52

        # @pane-left
        {
            The split layout renders two child scopes as panes, each with its
            own layout. Give it `weights: 48 52` to set the ratio.

            A pane is a slide within a slide: it can be a pipeline, a set of
            rows, a matrix, or plain prose like this one.
        }

        # @pane-right
        {
            config: rows

            variant: mono

            # Sep 2026 @t1
            {
                First milestone, stated as a date and an outcome.
            }

            # Mar 2027 @t2
            {
                Second milestone.
            }

            # Beta @t3
            {
                A stage name reads as well as a date does.
            }
        }
    }

    # Bars @bars-slide
    {
        config: bars

        kicker: Bars

        lede: Bars scale against the largest value unless a bar sets `fill:` itself.

        # Documents indexed @bar-1
        {
            value: 4200

            A note sets in monospace under the track.
        }

        # Documents reviewed @bar-2
        {
            value: 2150

            Values are parsed for their first number, so `4,200 docs` works too.
        }

        # Awaiting review @bar-3
        {
            value: 1780

            A bar with no `fill:` is scaled against the largest value.
        }

        # Rejected @bar-4
        {
            value: 270

            A bar scaled against the peak.
        }

        # Coverage @bar-5
        {
            value: about three quarters

            fill: 75

            No number to measure, so this bar sets `fill: 75` and draws itself.
            Without it the bar would be empty: the automatic scale has nothing
            to read.
        }
    }

    # Stacking two layouts @stack-slide
    {
        config: stack

        rule: true

        kicker: Stack

        lede: A stack renders each child scope as a block with its own layout, which is how one slide carries a diagram above a set of columns.

        # @stack-pipe
        {
            config: pipeline

            arrow: ⇒

            # Signal path @stack-row
            {
                accent: secondary

                {[.]
                    - Sense
                    - **Convert**
                    - Compute
                    - **Convert**
                    - Act
                }
            }
        }

        # @stack-cols
        {
            config: columns

            numbered: true

            # The engine @sc-1
            {
                Blocks compose downward, separated by a hairline when the stack
                sets `rule: true`.
            }

            # The path @sc-2
            {
                Each block reads its own `config:` line.
            }

            # The tooling @sc-3
            {
                Nesting stops here: a block inside a block is plain content.
            }
        }
    }

    # The default layout @default-slide
    {
        kicker: No config line

        A slide with no `config:` line is left-aligned and runs top to bottom.
        It is the layout every deck had before the structured ones existed, and
        it is unchanged.

        {[.]
            - Prose, lists and tables render as they always did
            - No child scope is consumed as structure
            - A nested scope is a nested scope, not a column
        }

        # A nested scope @default-nested
        {
            Under the default layout this renders inline as a section, rather
            than being read as a cell.
        }
    }

    # Two columns @two-column-slide
    {
        config: two-column

        kicker: Legacy layout

        lede: `two-column` predates `columns` and still behaves the way it did. Child scopes become the columns.

        # Before @tc-before
        {
            The original slide layouts were `center` and `two-column`. Themes
            written against them target the bare class name.
        }

        # After @tc-after
        {
            `columns` is the same idea with a count class, per-column accents,
            numbering and variants. Both are supported.
        }

        # Still a drilldown @tc-detail :detail
        {
            A detail slide hangs off any layout, including this one. Press Down
            to reach it, Up to come back.

            A `:detail` scope has to be a direct child of the slide. Nested
            inside a column it is not a drilldown at all — it renders inline as
            part of that column, which is easy to do by accident.
        }
    }

    # Everything still works @closing
    {
        config: center

        The unstructured layouts — default, `center`, `two-column` — behave
        exactly as they always did.

        # Notes @notes
        {
            Speaker notes, drilldown details and PDF export are unchanged.
            This deck exercises every layout, every variant and every slide
            property, so a theme can be checked against the whole set at once.
        }
    }
}
